This session made me realise that product management starts with understanding the real customer problem, not with deciding which technology to use.
The electricity outage case was a good example because initially it looked like a simple customer-support problem.
But when we looked at the complete customer journey, the problem became much broader.

Another thing I noticed was how important existing data and systems are before designing an AI solution.
For example, an AI voice assistant becomes much more useful when it can connect with operational systems and provide real-time information.

The discussion around pricing was also interesting.
Customers may not necessarily pay separately for an AI feature if they are actually paying for the main service.
So, as a PM, I need to understand where the real value is created and who is willing to pay for it.

Another important takeaway was that product decisions have to consider business, customer, technology and regulatory constraints together.
A technically impressive solution may still fail if it does not fit the customer's actual journey or business model.

I also understood why the PM needs to challenge assumptions instead of simply accepting them.
Overall, this session helped me connect the earlier concepts of customer problem, AI opportunity, value and product design into one practical example.

Key take away: before asking, Where can I use AI?, I should first ask “What is the customer's real problem, and what outcome are we trying to improve?”
